Title: Understanding Eating Disorders: Anorexia and Bulimia

Eating disorders, such as anorexia and bulimia, are serious mental and physical health conditions that can have a profound impact on an individual's life. They often develop during adolescence or young adulthood and affect approximately 0.9% to 4.1% of women and 0.3% to 1.5% of men (National Eating Disorders Association, 2021).

Anorexia is characterized by self-starvation and extreme weight loss, often due to an intense fear of gaining weight or becoming overweight. Bulimia involves recurrent episodes of eating large quantities of food (binging) followed by inappropriate compensatory behaviors to prevent weight gain, like self-induced vomiting, excessive exercise, or laxative abuse.

Both anorexia and bulimia can lead to numerous complications: malnutrition, weakened immune system, heart problems, gastrointestinal issues, and bone density loss

